<file path="etc/suricata/README.md">
  1: # NFTBan Suricata Configuration Files
  2: 
  3: ## File Organization
  4: 
  5: This directory contains Suricata IDS configuration files optimized for NFTBan v1.0.
  6: 
  7: ### Configuration Files Structure
  8: 
  9: ```
 10: etc/suricata/
 11: ├── filters.conf                    # NFTBan filter config (DEFAULT - don't edit)
 12: ├── filters.conf.local.example      # Example user overrides for filters
 13: ├── enable.conf                     # Suricata rules to enable (DEFAULT - don't edit)
 14: ├── enable.conf.local.example       # Example user additions for enabled rules
 15: ├── disable.conf                    # Suricata rules to disable (DEFAULT - don't edit)
 16: ├── disable.conf.local.example      # Example user additions for disabled rules
 17: └── rules/
 18:     └── local.rules                 # Custom Suricata rules (user-editable)
 19: ```
 20: 
 21: ---
 22: 
 23: ## Configuration Pattern
 24: 
 25: ### ⚠️ **DEFAULT files (.conf)**
 26: - Managed by NFTBan
 27: - **Will be overwritten on updates**
 28: - **DO NOT EDIT these files**
 29: 
 30: ### ✅ **LOCAL files (.conf.local)**
 31: - User customizations
 32: - **Never overwritten**
 33: - **Safe to edit**
 34: 
 35: ### 📋 **EXAMPLE files (.conf.local.example)**
 36: - Templates showing usage examples
 37: - Copy to `.conf.local` to activate
 38: 
 39: ---
 40: 
 41: ## Files Explained
 42: 
 43: ### 1. **filters.conf** (NFTBan Filter Configuration)
 44: **Purpose:** Controls how NFTBan processes Suricata alerts
 45: 
 46: **Features:**
 47: - Score thresholds for banning
 48: - Ban durations (30m, 1h, 24h, etc.)
 49: - Escalation policies (temporary → permanent)
 50: - Action modes (log, observe, ban)
 51: - Keywords for matching alerts
 52: 
 53: **Default Status:** Ships with balanced defaults for SSH, web, malware, botnet detection
 54: 
 55: **User Customization:**
 56: ```bash
 57: # Copy example to create your local config
 58: cp filters.conf.local.example filters.conf.local
 59: 
 60: # Edit with your custom thresholds, ban times, etc.
 61: nano filters.conf.local
 62: ```
 63: 
 64: **Example Override:**
 65: ```ini
 66: [filters]
 67: # Make SSH protection more aggressive
 68: ssh = true | ssh,brute | 70 | 20m | ban | escalate:2:12h | SSH (stricter)
 69: 
 70: # Enable FTP protection (disabled by default)
 71: ftp = true | ftp,vsftpd | 85 | 1h | ban | escalate:3:24h | FTP attacks
 72: ```
 73: 
 74: ---
 75: 
 76: ### 2. **enable.conf** (Suricata Rule Enablement)
 77: **Purpose:** Defines which Suricata detection rules to enable
 78: 
 79: **Default Configuration:**
 80: - ✅ SSH protection (brute-force, attacks)
 81: - ✅ Web exploits (SQLi, XSS, RFI, LFI)
 82: - ✅ WordPress attacks
 83: - ✅ Port scanning
 84: - ✅ Botnet detection (Mirai, Gafgyt, XORDDoS, etc.)
 85: - ✅ Cryptominers
 86: - ✅ DNS attacks
 87: - ✅ TLS monitoring
 88: - ✅ Exploit detection
 89: - ❌ Mail (SMTP/IMAP/POP3) - DISABLED by default
 90: - ❌ FTP - DISABLED by default
 91: - ❌ Database (MySQL/PostgreSQL) - DISABLED by default
 92: 
 93: **Resource Impact:** ~5-15% CPU, ~300-500 MB RAM (minimal profile)
 94: 
 95: **User Customization:**
 96: ```bash
 97: # Copy example to create your local config
 98: cp enable.conf.local.example enable.conf.local
 99: 
100: # Edit to add services you run
101: nano enable.conf.local
102: ```
103: 
104: **Example Additions:**
105: ```bash
106: # Enable mail protection (if you run Postfix/Dovecot)
107: re:smtp
108: re:SMTP
109: re:imap
110: re:IMAP
111: 
112: # Enable FTP protection
113: re:ftp
114: re:FTP
115: 
116: # Enable database protection
117: re:mysql
118: re:MySQL
119: ```
120: 
121: ---
122: 
123: ### 3. **disable.conf** (Suricata Rule Disablement)
124: **Purpose:** Defines which Suricata detection rules to disable (reduce noise)
125: 
126: **Default Disabled:**
127: - ❌ Windows protocols (SMB, DCERPC, RDP)
128: - ❌ Database protocols (MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.)
129: - ❌ VoIP/SIP
130: - ❌ SCADA/ICS
131: - ❌ Chat/P2P/Gaming
132: - ❌ Streaming media
133: - ❌ Policy rules (corporate monitoring)
134: - ❌ Informational alerts
135: 
136: **Resource Savings:** ~20-40% CPU reduction by disabling unused protocols
137: 
138: **User Customization:**
139: ```bash
140: # Copy example to create your local config
141: cp disable.conf.local.example disable.conf.local
142: 
143: # Edit to disable additional noisy rules
144: nano disable.conf.local
145: ```
146: 
147: **Example Additions:**
148: ```bash
149: # Disable DNS query noise (if too many alerts)
150: re:ET DNS query
151: re:DNS query for
152: 
153: # Disable scan alerts (if you run Nmap internally)
154: re:ET SCAN
155: re:port scan
156: 
157: # Disable specific noisy rule by SID
158: 2001219  # ET SCAN SSH BruteForce Tool
159: ```
160: 
161: ---
162: 
163: ### 4. **local.rules** (Custom Suricata Rules)
164: **Purpose:** Your custom Suricata detection signatures
165: 
166: **Location:** `/etc/suricata/rules/local.rules`
167: 
168: **Example Rules:**
169: ```
170: # Detect outbound SSH brute-forcing (kthreadadd64-style malware)
171: alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> any 22 (msg:"LOCAL BOTNET Outbound SSH Brute Force"; \
172:   flow:to_server,established; content:"SSH-"; depth:4; \
173:   threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 10, seconds 60; \
174:   sid:9000001; rev:1;)
175: 
176: # Detect WordPress wp-login.php brute-force
177: alert http any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"LOCAL WEB WordPress Login Brute Force"; \
178:   flow:to_server,established; http.uri; content:"/wp-login.php"; \
179:   threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 10, seconds 60; \
180:   sid:9000002; rev:1;)
181: ```
182: 
183: **User Customization:**
184: - Fully user-editable
185: - Never overwritten
186: - Use SID range 9000000-9999999 for local rules
187: 
188: ---
189: 
190: ## Usage Examples
191: 
192: ### Scenario 1: VPS/Cloud Server (Minimal)
193: **Services:** SSH + Nginx
194: 
195: **Configuration:**
196: 1. Use default `filters.conf` (no changes needed)
197: 2. Use default `enable.conf` (covers SSH + web)
198: 3. Use default `disable.conf` (disables unused services)
199: 
200: **Result:** 5-15% CPU, 300-500 MB RAM
201: 
202: ---
203: 
204: ### Scenario 2: cPanel/DirectAdmin Hosting
205: **Services:** SSH, Nginx, Apache, Postfix, Dovecot, FTP
206: 
207: **Configuration:**
208: 1. Keep default `filters.conf`
209: 2. Create `enable.conf.local`:
210:    ```bash
211:    # Enable mail protection
212:    re:smtp
213:    re:SMTP
214:    re:imap
215:    re:IMAP
216: 
217:    # Enable FTP protection
218:    re:ftp
219:    re:FTP
220:    ```
221: 3. Create `filters.conf.local`:
222:    ```ini
223:    [filters]
224:    mail = true | smtp,imap,pop3 | 90 | 45m | ban | escalate:3:24h | Mail abuse
225:    ftp  = true | ftp,vsftpd | 85 | 1h | ban | escalate:3:24h | FTP attacks
226:    ```
227: 
228: **Result:** 15-30% CPU, 500-800 MB RAM
229: 
230: ---
231: 
232: ### Scenario 3: High-Security Server
233: **Services:** All protections enabled
234: 
235: **Configuration:**
236: 1. Keep default `filters.conf`
237: 2. Create `enable.conf.local`:
238:    ```bash
239:    # Enable everything
240:    re:smtp
241:    re:ftp
242:    re:mysql
243:    re:ET TROJAN
244:    re:ransomware
245:    re:exploit.*kit
246:    ```
247: 3. Tune `filters.conf.local` for aggressive banning
248: 
249: **Result:** 30-50% CPU, 800MB-1.2GB RAM
250: 
251: ---
252: 
253: ## Applying Configuration Changes
254: 
255: ### Step 1: Update Suricata Rules
256: ```bash
257: # If you have .local files, include them:
258: suricata-update \
259:   --enable-conf /etc/suricata/enable.conf \
260:   --enable-conf /etc/suricata/enable.conf.local \
261:   --disable-conf /etc/suricata/disable.conf \
262:   --disable-conf /etc/suricata/disable.conf.local
263: 
264: # If you only use defaults:
265: suricata-update \
266:   --enable-conf /etc/suricata/enable.conf \
267:   --disable-conf /etc/suricata/disable.conf
268: ```
269: 
270: ### Step 2: Test Configuration
271: ```bash
272: # Validate suricata.yaml syntax
273: suricata -T -c /etc/suricata/suricata.yaml
274: 
275: # Check how many rules loaded
276: grep "rule reload complete" /var/log/suricata/suricata.log
277: ```
278: 
279: ### Step 3: Restart Suricata
280: ```bash
281: # Apply changes
282: systemctl restart suricata
283: 
284: # Verify running
285: systemctl status suricata
286: ```
287: 
288: ### Step 4: Monitor Resource Usage
289: ```bash
290: # CPU and RAM usage
291: systemd-cgtop | grep suricata
292: ps aux | grep suricata
293: 
294: # Check alerts
295: tail -f /var/log/suricata/eve.json | jq 'select(.event_type=="alert")'
296: ```
297: 
298: ---
299: 
300: ## Troubleshooting
301: 
302: ### Too Many Alerts?
303: 1. Add noisy patterns to `disable.conf.local`
304: 2. Increase thresholds in `filters.conf.local`
305: 3. Switch action from `ban` to `observe` temporarily
306: 
307: ### Too Few Alerts?
308: 1. Lower thresholds in `filters.conf.local`
309: 2. Add more patterns to `enable.conf.local`
310: 3. Check Suricata is actually running and processing traffic
311: 
312: ### High CPU Usage?
313: 1. Disable unused protocols in `disable.conf.local`
314: 2. Reduce enabled rules in `enable.conf.local`
315: 3. Check `/install/templates/suricata.yaml.optimized` for tuning
316: 
317: ### False Positives?
318: 1. Identify rule SID causing false positive
319: 2. Add SID to `disable.conf.local`
320: 3. Or adjust threshold in `filters.conf.local`
321: 
322: ---
323: 
324: ## Performance Profiles
325: 
326: | Profile | CPU | RAM | Rules Enabled | Use Case |
327: |---------|-----|-----|---------------|----------|
328: | **Minimal** | 5-15% | 300-500 MB | SSH, Web, Scan, Botnet, Miner | VPS/Cloud |
329: | **Standard** | 15-30% | 500-800 MB | + Mail, FTP, DNS | cPanel/DA |
330: | **Maximum** | 30-50% | 800MB-1.2GB | + Trojan, Exploit kits, All | High-security |
331: 
332: ---
333: 
334: ## Best Practices
335: 
336: 1. **Start Minimal:** Use defaults, only add what you need
337: 2. **Test in Stages:**
338:    - `action=log` (testing)
339:    - `action=observe` (tuning)
340:    - `action=ban` (production)
341: 3. **Use Escalation:** `escalate:3:24h` instead of permanent immediately
342: 4. **Monitor Regularly:** Check `eve.json` for alert patterns
343: 5. **Keep .local Files:** Never edit default configs directly
344: 6. **Document Changes:** Comment your .local files explaining why
345: 
346: ---
347: 
348: ## See Also
349: 
350: - **Suricata YAML Optimization:** `/install/templates/suricata.yaml.optimized`
351: - **Installation Script:** `/cli/lib/nftban/setup/install_suricata.sh`
352: - **Migration Guide:** `/docs/migration/FAIL2BAN_TO_SURICATA_MIGRATION.md`
353: - **NFTBan Filters:** `/etc/suricata/filters.conf`
354: 
355: ---
356: 
357: **Last Updated:** 2025-11-29
358: **NFTBan Version:** 1.0.0
</file>

