  1: #!/bin/bash
  2: # =============================================================================
  3: # NFTBan Test Server Cleanup Script
  4: # =============================================================================
  5: # Removes NFTBan completely from a test server for clean reinstall testing
  6: #
  7: # Usage: ssh root@server 'bash -s' < scripts/test_server_cleanup.sh
  8: #        OR: ./scripts/test_server_cleanup.sh (run on server directly)
  9: #
 10: # =============================================================================
 11: 
 12: set -euo pipefail
 13: 
 14: RED='\033[0;31m'
 15: GREEN='\033[0;32m'
 16: YELLOW='\033[1;33m'
 17: NC='\033[0m'
 18: 
 19: log()  { echo -e "${GREEN}[CLEANUP]${NC} $*"; }
 20: warn() { echo -e "${YELLOW}[WARN]${NC} $*"; }
 21: err()  { echo -e "${RED}[ERROR]${NC} $*"; }
 22: 
 23: echo "=============================================="
 24: echo "NFTBan Test Server Cleanup"
 25: echo "=============================================="
 26: echo ""
 27: 
 28: # Check root
 29: if [[ $EUID -ne 0 ]]; then
 30:     err "Must run as root"
 31:     exit 1
 32: fi
 33: 
 34: # Stop services
 35: log "Stopping services..."
 36: systemctl stop nftban-ui 2>/dev/null || true
 37: systemctl stop nftban-health.timer 2>/dev/null || true
 38: systemctl stop nftban 2>/dev/null || true
 39: systemctl disable nftban-ui 2>/dev/null || true
 40: systemctl disable nftban-health.timer 2>/dev/null || true
 41: systemctl disable nftban 2>/dev/null || true
 42: 
 43: # Remove nftables rules
 44: log "Removing nftables rules..."
 45: nft delete table ip nftban 2>/dev/null || true
 46: nft delete table ip6 nftban 2>/dev/null || true
 47: 
 48: # Remove binaries
 49: log "Removing binaries..."
 50: rm -f /usr/bin/nftban
 51: rm -f /usr/sbin/nftban-ui
 52: rm -f /usr/sbin/nftban-core
 53: rm -f /usr/libexec/nftban-ui-auth
 54: rm -rf /usr/lib/nftban/bin/
 55: 
 56: # Remove libraries
 57: log "Removing libraries..."
 58: rm -rf /usr/lib/nftban/
 59: 
 60: # Remove configs (backup first)
 61: log "Backing up and removing configs..."
 62: if [[ -d /etc/nftban ]]; then
 63:     cp -r /etc/nftban "/etc/nftban.backup.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)" 2>/dev/null || true
 64:     rm -rf /etc/nftban
 65: fi
 66: 
 67: # Remove data directories
 68: log "Removing data directories..."
 69: rm -rf /var/lib/nftban
 70: rm -rf /var/log/nftban
 71: rm -rf /var/cache/nftban
 72: rm -rf /run/nftban
 73: 
 74: # Remove systemd files
 75: log "Removing systemd files..."
 76: rm -f /etc/systemd/system/nftban*.service
 77: rm -f /etc/systemd/system/nftban*.timer
 78: rm -f /usr/lib/systemd/system/nftban*.service
 79: rm -f /usr/lib/systemd/system/nftban*.timer
 80: rm -f /etc/tmpfiles.d/nftban.conf
 81: rm -f /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/nftban.conf
 82: 
 83: # Remove polkit
 84: log "Removing polkit rules..."
 85: rm -f /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/com.nftban.*
 86: rm -f /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/*nftban*
 87: rm -f /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/*nftban*
 88: 
 89: # Remove bash completion
 90: log "Removing bash completion..."
 91: rm -f /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/nftban
 92: rm -f /etc/bash_completion.d/nftban
 93: 
 94: # Remove user/group (optional - comment out if you want to keep)
 95: log "Removing nftban user/group..."
 96: userdel nftban 2>/dev/null || true
 97: groupdel nftban 2>/dev/null || true
 98: groupdel nftban-auditors 2>/dev/null || true
 99: # Legacy cleanup (v0.x had nftban-web group)
100: groupdel nftban-web 2>/dev/null || true
101: 
102: # Reload systemd
103: log "Reloading systemd..."
104: systemctl daemon-reload
105: 
106: # Remove test repo if exists
107: log "Removing test repo..."
108: rm -rf /root/nftban-dev
109: rm -rf /root/nftban-v1.0-dev
110: rm -rf /tmp/nftban*
111: 
112: echo ""
113: echo "=============================================="
114: echo "Cleanup complete!"
115: echo "=============================================="
116: echo ""
117: echo "Server is clean. Ready for fresh install test."
118: echo ""
119: echo "To test install:"
120: echo "  git clone <repo> /path/to/nftban"
121: echo "  cd /path/to/nftban"
122: echo "  ./install.sh cli    # or: ./install.sh all"
123: echo ""
</file>

<file path="wiki/Suricata-Integration.md">
  1: # Suricata IDS/IPS Integration
  2: 
  3: NFTBan v1.0+ includes comprehensive Suricata integration with intelligent rule management, performance profiling, and real-time statistics.
  4: 
  5: ## Table of Contents
  6: 
  7: - [Overview](#overview)
  8: - [Features](#features)
  9: - [Quick Start](#quick-start)
 10: - [Installation](#installation)
 11: - [Profile System](#profile-system)
 12: - [Service-Based Rule Filtering](#service-based-rule-filtering)
 13: - [SID Statistics Engine](#sid-statistics-engine)
 14: - [Custom Rules Management](#custom-rules-management)
 15: - [Recommendations Engine](#recommendations-engine)
 16: - [CLI Commands Reference](#cli-commands-reference)
 17: - [Testing Guide](#testing-guide)
 18: - [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)
 19: 
 20: ---
 21: 
 22: ## Overview
 23: 
 24: NFTBan's Suricata integration provides:
 25: 
 26: - **Automatic Performance Profiling** - Detects system resources and applies optimal configuration
 27: - **Service-Based Rule Filtering** - Reduces loaded rules by 50-70% based on detected services
 28: - **Real-Time Statistics** - Tracks SID triggers, false positives, and attack patterns
 29: - **Custom Rules Manager** - Safe CRUD operations with validation and rollback
 30: - **Intelligent Recommendations** - Analyzes patterns and suggests optimizations
 31: 
 32: **Architecture:**
 33: ```
 34: ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 35: │ NFTBan Suricata Integration                                 │
 36: ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 37: │ Profile System     │ Auto-detect CPU/RAM → Apply profile    │
 38: │ Service Scanner    │ Scan localhost → Filter rules          │
 39: │ Rules Manager      │ Generate enabled.list (3-layer config) │
 40: │ Stats Engine       │ Parse eve.json → Track SIDs            │
 41: │ Custom Rules       │ CRUD + Validation + Backup/Rollback    │
 42: │ Recommendations    │ Analyze patterns → Optimize ruleset    │
 43: └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
 44: ```
 45: 
 46: ---
 47: 
 48: ## Features
 49: 
 50: ### 1. Performance Profiles
 51: 
 52: Three pre-configured profiles optimized for different resource levels:
 53: 
 54: | Profile | CPU Cores | RAM | Use Case |
 55: |---------|-----------|-----|----------|
 56: | **Minimal** | 2 | 2 GB | Tiny VPS, stability first |
 57: | **Standard** | 4 | 4-8 GB | Most servers (default) |
 58: | **Maximum** | 8+ | 8-16 GB | High traffic, deep inspection |
 59: 
 60: **Key Settings:**
 61: - Ring buffer size (50k/100k/300k)
 62: - Flow timeouts (60s/120s/300s)
 63: - HTTP body inspection depth
 64: - Detection engine tuning
 65: 
 66: ### 2. Service-Based Rule Filtering
 67: 
 68: **Problem:** Suricata loads 30,000-40,000 rules by default, wasting CPU/RAM on irrelevant signatures.
 69: 
 70: **Solution:** Scan localhost services → Enable only relevant rule categories.
 71: 
 72: **Results:**
 73: - 50-70% reduction in loaded rules
 74: - Faster startup times
 75: - Lower memory usage
 76: - Same security for your actual services
 77: 
 78: **How it Works:**
 79: ```
 80: 1. Scan localhost ports (HTTP:80, SSH:22, MySQL:3306, etc.)
 81: 2. Map services → Suricata categories (http, ssh, mysql, etc.)
 82: 3. Generate auto.conf with detected categories
 83: 4. Merge with local.conf (user overrides) → effective.conf
 84: 5. Create enabled.list for Suricata
 85: ```
 86: 
 87: ### 3. SID Statistics Engine
 88: 
 89: Real-time monitoring of Suricata signature triggers:
 90: 
 91: - **Prometheus Metrics** - Time-series data for dashboards
 92: - **In-Memory Cache** - Fast CLI queries without database overhead
 93: - **JSON Snapshots** - Persist across restarts
 94: - **Historical Analysis** - Track patterns over time
 95: 
 96: **Tracked Metrics:**
 97: - Total triggers per SID
 98: - Unique source IPs
 99: - First/Last trigger timestamps
100: - Category aggregates
101: - Alert severity levels
102: 
103: ### 4. Custom Rules Management
104: 
105: Safe custom rule operations with SID range enforcement:
106: 
107: - **SID Range:** 9000000-9999999 (prevents conflicts with official rulesets)
108: - **Validation:** Syntax checking before adding/editing
109: - **Automatic Backups:** Created before every modification (last 10 kept)
110: - **Safe Rollback:** Restore previous state at any time
111: - **Enable/Disable:** Comment/uncomment without deletion
112: 
113: ### 5. Recommendations Engine
114: 
115: Intelligent analysis of SID patterns with actionable suggestions:
116: 
117: **Detection Patterns:**
118: 
119: | Type | Pattern | Recommendation |
120: |------|---------|----------------|
121: | **False Positive** | 100+ triggers from 1-3 sources | Review threshold, likely misconfiguration |
122: | **Noise Reduction** | 1000+ triggers, widespread sources | Tighten rule conditions |
123: | **Drop Mode** | Attack pattern from many sources | Switch from alert to drop/block |
124: | **Disable Rule** | 10,000+ triggers, very recent | Investigate and disable if needed |
125: 
126: ---
127: 
128: ## Quick Start
129: 
130: ### One-Time Setup (5 minutes)
131: 
132: ```bash
133: # 1. Auto-detect and apply profile
134: nftban suricata profile detect
135: nftban suricata profile set standard
136: 
137: # 2. Scan services and generate optimized rules
138: nftban suricata scan
139: nftban suricata rules init
140: nftban suricata rules generate
141: 
142: # 3. Restart Suricata with new config
143: sudo systemctl restart suricata
144: 
145: # 4. Start statistics collector (optional)
146: sudo systemctl enable --now nftban-suricata-stats
147: ```
148: 
149: ### Daily Operations
150: 
151: ```bash
152: # Check what's triggering
153: nftban suricata sid top
154: 
155: # Get optimization recommendations
156: nftban suricata recommend
157: 
158: # Add custom rules
159: nftban suricata custom add 'alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg:"Custom Rule"; sid:9000000; rev:1;)'
160: 
161: # Monitor recent activity
162: nftban suricata sid recent
163: ```
164: 
165: ---
166: 
167: ## Installation
168: 
169: ### Prerequisites
170: 
171: ```bash
172: # Install Suricata
173: sudo dnf install suricata     # RHEL/Rocky/Alma
174: sudo apt install suricata     # Debian/Ubuntu
175: 
176: # Install suricata-update (for rules)
177: sudo pip3 install --upgrade suricata-update
178: ```
179: 
180: ### NFTBan Installation
181: 
182: ```bash
183: # 1. Install NFTBan (if not already installed)
184: sudo dnf install nftban       # RPM
185: sudo dpkg -i nftban*.deb      # DEB
186: 
187: # 2. Verify installation
188: nftban suricata help
189: 
190: # 3. Initialize Suricata integration
191: nftban suricata rules init
192: ```
193: 
194: ### Directory Structure
195: 
196: ```
197: /etc/nftban/suricata/
198: ├── profiles/
199: │   ├── minimal.yaml      # 2 cores / 2GB RAM
200: │   ├── standard.yaml     # 4 cores / 4-8GB RAM
201: │   └── maximum.yaml      # 8+ cores / 8-16GB RAM
202: ├── config/
203: │   ├── suricata.local.conf      # User manual settings (never overwritten)
204: │   ├── suricata.auto.conf       # Auto-generated from scan
205: │   ├── suricata.effective.conf  # Merged result (local + auto)
206: │   ├── enabled.list             # Generated rules list for Suricata
207: │   └── profile.conf             # Active profile setting
208: ├── rules/
209: │   ├── custom.rules             # Your custom rules (SID 9000000-9999999)
210: │   └── backups/                 # Automatic backups
211: │       └── custom.rules.YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS
212: └── cache/
213:     └── sid-stats.json           # SID statistics cache
214: ```
215: 
216: ---
217: 
218: ## Profile System
219: 
220: ### Auto-Detection
221: 
222: ```bash
223: # Detect optimal profile based on CPU and RAM
224: nftban suricata profile detect
225: ```
226: 
227: **Output:**
228: ```
229: CPU Cores: 4
230: RAM: 8 GB
231: Recommended Profile: standard
232: ```
233: 
234: ### Apply Profile
235: 
236: ```bash
237: # Apply profile (minimal/standard/maximum)
238: nftban suricata profile set standard
239: ```
240: 
241: **What it Does:**
242: 1. Copies profile YAML to `/etc/suricata/suricata.yaml`
243: 2. Updates `/etc/nftban/suricata/config/profile.conf`
244: 3. Configures ring buffers, timeouts, inspection depths
245: 
246: ### View Current Profile
247: 
248: ```bash
249: # Show active profile settings
250: nftban suricata profile show
251: ```
252: 
253: ### Validate Profile
254: 
255: ```bash
256: # Check profile configuration
257: nftban suricata profile validate
258: ```
259: 
260: ---
261: 
262: ## Service-Based Rule Filtering
263: 
264: ### 3-Layer Configuration System
265: 
266: **Layer 1: Auto-Detection** (`suricata.auto.conf`)
267: - Generated automatically from service scan
268: - **Never manually edited**
269: - Overwritten on each scan
270: 
271: **Layer 2: User Overrides** (`suricata.local.conf`)
272: - Manual user settings
273: - **Never overwritten by NFTBan**
274: - Takes precedence over auto.conf
275: 
276: **Layer 3: Effective Config** (`suricata.effective.conf`)
277: - Merged result: local.conf + auto.conf
278: - Generated automatically
279: - Used to create enabled.list
280: 
281: ### Quick Scan
282: 
283: ```bash
284: # Fast port scan of localhost
285: nftban suricata scan
286: ```
287: 
288: **Detects:**
289: - Common ports (21, 22, 25, 53, 80, 443, 3306, etc.)
290: - Maps to Suricata categories
291: 
292: **Output:**
293: ```
294: Detected Services:
295:   - HTTP (80) → http, web-application
296:   - HTTPS (443) → tls, ssl, web-application
297:   - SSH (22) → ssh
298:   - MySQL (3306) → mysql
299: 
300: Auto-conf generated: /etc/nftban/suricata/config/suricata.auto.conf
301: ```
302: 
303: ### Deep Scan
304: 
305: ```bash
306: # Deep scan with protocol probes
307: nftban suricata scan deep
308: ```
309: 
310: **Probes:**
311: - **HTTP:** Send GET request, verify response
312: - **HTTPS:** TLS handshake, verify certificate
313: - **SSH:** Read banner, verify SSH protocol
314: - **MySQL:** Protocol handshake, version detection
315: - **DNS:** Query test, verify response
316: 
317: ### Generate Rules List
318: 
319: ```bash
320: # Create enabled.list from effective config
321: nftban suricata rules generate
322: ```
323: 
324: **Process:**
325: 1. Merges local.conf + auto.conf → effective.conf
326: 2. Filters rule files by enabled categories
327: 3. Applies whitelist/blacklist
328: 4. Writes enabled.list for Suricata
329: 
330: ### View Statistics
331: 
332: ```bash
333: # Show rule reduction statistics
334: nftban suricata rules stats
335: ```
336: 
337: **Output:**
338: ```
339: Rules Statistics:
340:   Total rule files: 245
341:   Enabled rule files: 82 (33.5%)
342:   Rule reduction: 66.5%
343: 
344: Enabled Categories:
345:   - http (42 files)
346:   - tls (18 files)
347:   - ssh (12 files)
348:   - mysql (10 files)
349: ```
350: 
351: ### Manual Overrides
352: 
353: Edit `/etc/nftban/suricata/config/suricata.local.conf`:
354: 
355: ```ini
356: # Enable additional categories (not detected by scan)
357: enabled_categories = dns,smtp,ftp
358: 
359: # Disable specific categories
360: disabled_categories = icmp
361: 
362: # Whitelist specific rule files
363: whitelist = emerging-exploit.rules
364: 
365: # Blacklist specific rule files
366: blacklist = emerging-info.rules
367: ```
368: 
369: Then regenerate:
370: ```bash
371: nftban suricata rules generate
372: sudo systemctl reload suricata
373: ```
374: 
375: ---
376: 
377: ## SID Statistics Engine
378: 
379: ### Overview Statistics
380: 
381: ```bash
382: # Show overall SID statistics
383: nftban suricata sid stats
384: ```
385: 
386: **Output:**
387: ```
388: SID Statistics:
389:   Total SIDs tracked: 347
390:   Total triggers: 15,432
391:   Unique sources: 89
392: 
393: Cache: /etc/nftban/suricata/cache/sid-stats.json
394: ```
395: 
396: ### Top Triggered SIDs
397: 
398: ```bash
399: # Show top 20 SIDs by trigger count
400: nftban suricata sid top
401: ```
402: 
403: **Output:**
404: ```
405: Rank  SID       Triggers  Sources  Category          Signature
406: ----  --------  --------  -------  ----------------  --------------------
407: 1     2100498   3,421     12       web-application   SQL Injection Attempt
408: 2     2100356   2,103     45       exploit           Buffer Overflow
409: 3     2100287   1,892     8        trojan            Malware Callback
410: ...
411: ```
412: 
413: ### SID Details
414: 
415: ```bash
416: # Detailed info for specific SID
417: nftban suricata sid info 2100498
418: ```
419: 
420: **Output:**
421: ```
422: SID: 2100498
423: Category: web-application
424: Signature: ET WEB_SERVER SQL Injection Attempt
425: 
426: Trigger Count: 3,421
427: First Trigger: 2025-12-25 08:15:32
428: Last Trigger: 2025-12-31 14:22:18
429: 
430: Unique Sources: 12
431: Top Source IPs:
432:   - 192.168.1.105 (1,203 triggers)
433:   - 10.0.0.45 (892 triggers)
434:   - 172.16.0.33 (645 triggers)
435:   ...
436: ```
437: 
438: ### Recent Activity
439: 
440: ```bash
441: # Show SIDs triggered in last 24 hours
442: nftban suricata sid recent
443: ```
444: 
445: **Output:**
446: ```
447: SID       Last Trigger  Triggers  Sources  Signature
448: --------  ------------  --------  -------  --------------------
449: 2100498   5m ago        234       3        SQL Injection Attempt
450: 2100356   2h ago        67        8        Buffer Overflow
451: 2100287   12h ago       45        2        Malware Callback
452: ...
453: ```
454: 
455: ### Statistics Daemon
456: 
457: Runs continuously to collect SID statistics from eve.json:
458: 
459: ```bash
460: # Start as systemd service
461: sudo systemctl start nftban-suricata-stats
462: sudo systemctl enable nftban-suricata-stats
463: 
464: # Or run manually (foreground)
465: sudo nftban-core suricata stats-daemon
466: ```
467: 
468: **Features:**
469: - Tails `/var/log/suricata/eve.json`
470: - Updates Prometheus metrics in real-time
471: - Saves cache snapshots every 5 minutes
472: - Graceful shutdown on SIGTERM/SIGINT
473: 
474: ---
475: 
476: ## Custom Rules Management
477: 
478: ### List Custom Rules
479: 
480: ```bash
481: # Show all custom rules
482: nftban suricata custom list
483: ```
484: 
485: **Output:**
486: ```
487: SID       Status      Action  Message
488: --------  ----------  ------  --------------------
489: 9000000   ✓ Enabled   alert   Test HTTP Traffic
490: 9000001   ✗ Disabled  drop    Block Malicious IP
491: ```
492: 
493: ### Add Custom Rule
494: 
495: ```bash
496: # Add new custom rule
497: nftban suricata custom add 'alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg:"HTTP Traffic Monitor"; sid:9000000; rev:1;)'
498: ```
499: 
500: **Validation Checks:**
501: - SID in range 9000000-9999999
502: - Valid syntax (action, protocol, msg, sid, rev)
503: - Unique SID (not already in use)
504: - Optional: Suricata binary validation (`suricata -T`)
505: 
506: **Output:**
507: ```
508: ✓ Custom rule added successfully
509: 
510: SID:     9000000
511: Action:  alert
512: Message: HTTP Traffic Monitor
513: 
514: Next steps:
515:   1. Reload Suricata: systemctl reload suricata
516:   2. Monitor alerts: nftban suricata sid info 9000000
517: ```
518: 
519: ### Edit Custom Rule
520: 
521: ```bash
522: # Update existing rule (must keep same SID)
