918: 
919: ## Performance Impact
920: 
921: ### Before Suricata Integration
922: 
923: - **Rules Loaded:** 30,000-40,000
924: - **Startup Time:** 15-30 seconds
925: - **Memory Usage:** 800MB-1.5GB
926: - **CPU Usage:** 20-40% of 1 core (idle)
927: 
928: ### After Suricata Integration
929: 
930: - **Rules Loaded:** 10,000-15,000 (50-70% reduction)
931: - **Startup Time:** 5-10 seconds
932: - **Memory Usage:** 300-600MB
933: - **CPU Usage:** 10-20% of 1 core (idle)
934: 
935: **Benchmark Command:**
936: ```bash
937: # Before
938: time sudo systemctl restart suricata
939: top -p $(pgrep suricata)
940: 
941: # Apply service filtering
942: nftban suricata scan
943: nftban suricata rules generate
944: sudo systemctl restart suricata
945: 
946: # After
947: time sudo systemctl restart suricata
948: top -p $(pgrep suricata)
949: ```
950: 
951: ---
952: 
953: ## Best Practices
954: 
955: 1. **Run Service Scan After Changes**
956:    - Added new services? Re-scan and regenerate rules
957:    - Removed services? Re-scan to reduce rules further
958: 
959: 2. **Review Recommendations Weekly**
960:    - Check for false positives
961:    - Optimize noisy rules
962:    - Consider drop mode for active attacks
963: 
964: 3. **Use Custom Rules Sparingly**
965:    - Only add rules for specific needs
966:    - Keep SID range organized (9000000-9000999 for type A, etc.)
967:    - Document rule purpose in `msg` field
968: 
969: 4. **Monitor Statistics Regularly**
970:    - Check `sid top` to spot anomalies
971:    - Investigate high-trigger SIDs
972:    - Review `sid recent` for active threats
973: 
974: 5. **Keep Backups**
975:    - Automatic backups are created, but verify they exist
976:    - Test rollback procedure before you need it
977:    - Keep custom.rules in version control
978: 
979: 6. **Profile Selection**
980:    - Start with auto-detected profile
981:    - Monitor performance under load
982:    - Adjust if needed (upsize for more inspection, downsize for stability)
983: 
